  "items": [
    {
      "id": "https://deepmind.com/alphafold-4",
      "url": "https://deepmind.com/alphafold-4",
      "title": "AlphaFold 4: full protein complex prediction including ligands + nucleic acids + lipids",
      "content_html": "<p>AlphaFold 4 is DeepMind&#x27;s 4th-generation protein structure predictor. The key upgrade: full complex prediction including small-molecule ligands, DNA, RNA, and lipids \u2014 not just proteins. The model uses a diffusion-based architecture with a unified tokeniser for all molecular types.</p><p>On CASP17, AlphaFold 4 achieves GDT_TS 0.94 for single chains (vs AF3 0.87, ESM-4 0.92) and 0.89 for multi-chain complexes (vs AF3 0.82). On the PoseBench-2027 benchmark (protein-ligand binding pose), AF4 achieves 0.93 Top-1 accuracy (vs AF3 0.85, AutoDock Vina 0.61).</p><p>Released via AlphaFold Server (free for non-commercial) + Isomorphic Cloud API (commercial). The model handles complexes up to 5000 residues + 50 ligands in a single prediction.</p>",
      "summary": "AlphaFold 4 is DeepMind's 4th-generation protein structure predictor. The key upgrade: full complex prediction including small-molecule ligands, DNA, RNA, and lipids \u2014 not just proteins. The model uses a diffusion-based architecture with a unified tokeniser for all molecular types.",
      "date_published": "2027-01-22T00:00:00Z",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "DeepMind Isomorphic Sciences"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "Biology - Protein structure",
        "DeepMind blog"
      ],
      "_aihpc_dataset": {
        "name": "CASP17 + PoseBench-2027 benchmarks",
        "format": "PDB + SDF",
        "size": "2.1 GB",
        "url": "https://github.com/deepmind/alphafold-4-2027"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "https://blogs.nvidia.com/earth-3-2027",
      "url": "https://blogs.nvidia.com/earth-3-2027",
      "title": "NVIDIA Earth-3: 10km global climate + 1km regional weather in a unified digital twin",
      "content_html": "<p>Earth-3 is NVIDIA&#x27;s 3rd-generation climate+weather digital twin. It unifies global climate simulation (10km resolution, 100-year projections) with regional weather forecasting (1km resolution, 14-day forecasts) in a single model \u2014 the first to bridge the climate-weather gap.</p><p>Climate mode: 100-year global projection at 10km in 6 hours (vs 6 months for CMIP6 models). Weather mode: 14-day regional forecast at 1km in 2 minutes (vs 45 minutes for HRRR). Both modes share the same PINN backbone (Modulus v3, 50B params).</p><p>Released via Earth-3 Cloud. 67 national weather services use the weather mode; 34 climate research institutions use the climate mode for IPCC AR8 contributions.</p>",
      "summary": "Earth-3 is NVIDIA's 3rd-generation climate+weather digital twin. It unifies global climate simulation (10km resolution, 100-year projections) with regional weather forecasting (1km resolution, 14-day forecasts) in a single model \u2014 the first to bridge the climate-weather gap.",
      "date_published": "2027-01-20T00:00:00Z",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "NVIDIA + NOAA + ECMWF + JMA"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "Physics - Climate + weather digital twin",
        "NVIDIA blog"
      ],
      "_aihpc_dataset": {
        "name": "Earth-3 benchmark (climate 10km \u00d7 100yr + weather 1km \u00d7 14day)",
        "format": "NetCDF + Zarr",
        "size": "24 TB",
        "url": "https://www.earth3.cloud/2027"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "https://anthropic.com/claude-5-ultra",
      "url": "https://anthropic.com/claude-5-ultra",
      "title": "Claude 5 Ultra: 3T-param reasoning model \u2014 solves Riemann hypothesis (conditional)",
      "content_html": "<p>Claude 5 Ultra is Anthropic&#x27;s 3-trillion-parameter (MoE 1024 experts, top-16 routing) ultra-reasoning model. The &#x27;Ultra&#x27; variant extends Claude 5 Opus&#x27;s thinking budget from 1M to 10M tokens, enabling multi-day deductive reasoning. Active params: ~120B per token.</p><p>On ARC-AGI-2, Claude 5 Ultra scores 0.91 (approaching the human-expert threshold of 0.95). On the Riemann Hypothesis Challenge (RH-2027), Claude 5 Ultra produced a 47-page proof that passes 8 of 10 verification checks \u2014 the closest any AI has come to solving a Millennium Prize problem.</p><p>The 2 failing checks are in the analytic continuation step \u2014 the proof assumes a conjecture about L-functions that is itself unproven. Anthropic estimates that Claude 6 (with 100M-token thinking) could complete the proof. Released via API; cost: $500 per 1M thinking tokens.</p>",
      "summary": "Claude 5 Ultra is Anthropic's 3-trillion-parameter (MoE 1024 experts, top-16 routing) ultra-reasoning model. The 'Ultra' variant extends Claude 5 Opus's thinking budget from 1M to 10M tokens, enabling multi-day deductive reasoning. Active params: ~120B per token.",
      "date_published": "2027-01-24T00:00:00Z",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Anthropic Research"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "CS - Ultra reasoning",
        "Anthropic blog"
      ],
      "_aihpc_dataset": {
        "name": "RH-2027 challenge + ARC-AGI-2 results",
        "format": "LaTeX + JSON",
        "size": "48 MB",
        "url": "https://github.com/anthropic/claude-5-ultra-2027"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "https://ai.meta.com/llama-5",
      "url": "https://ai.meta.com/llama-5",
      "title": "Llama 5: 1T-param open-weight MoE (Apache 2.0) \u2014 matches Claude 5 at 1/10th the cost",
      "content_html": "<p>Llama 5 is Meta&#x27;s 1-trillion-parameter (MoE 256 experts, top-8 routing) open-weight LLM. Released under Apache 2.0 \u2014 the largest fully-open model ever. On MMLU-Pro, Llama 5 scores 0.89 (matching Claude 5&#x27;s 0.89, beating GPT-5&#x27;s 0.85). On HumanEval-Plus, Llama 5 scores 0.93 (beating Claude 5&#x27;s 0.91).</p><p>The 256-expert MoE means only ~80B params active per token (top-8 of 256 experts \u00d7 ~4B/expert). Deployable on a single NVIDIA B200 (192 GB HBM3e). The model was trained on 15T tokens of curated text + code + math.</p><p>Released as 4 variants: Llama 5 Mini (8B, on-device), Llama 5 Small (70B), Llama 5 Medium (400B), Llama 5 Max (1T). All Apache 2.0. The Max variant matches closed models at 1/10th the API cost.</p>",
      "summary": "Llama 5 is Meta's 1-trillion-parameter (MoE 256 experts, top-8 routing) open-weight LLM. Released under Apache 2.0 \u2014 the largest fully-open model ever. On MMLU-Pro, Llama 5 scores 0.89 (matching Claude 5's 0.89, beating GPT-5's 0.85). On HumanEval-Plus, Llama 5 scores 0.93 (beating Claude 5's 0.91).",
      "date_published": "2027-01-23T00:00:00Z",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Meta AI"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "CS - Open-weight LLM",
        "Meta AI blog"
      ],
      "_aihpc_dataset": {
        "name": "MMLU-Pro + HumanEval-Plus benchmarks",
        "format": "JSON",
        "size": "120 MB",
        "url": "https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-5"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "https://research.ibm.com/condor-2",
      "url": "https://research.ibm.com/condor-2",
      "title": "IBM Condor 2: 100,000-qubit superconducting quantum computer with 1000 logical qubits",
      "content_html": "<p>IBM Condor 2 is a 100,000-qubit superconducting quantum processor (transmon, 4K dilution refrigerator). The world&#x27;s first 100k-qubit quantum computer. Modular architecture: 100 tiles of 1000 qubits each, coupled via cryogenic microwave links.</p><p>Condor 2 runs a distance-11 surface code (121 data + 120 ancilla = 241 qubits per logical qubit) achieving 10^-5 logical error rate. The 100k physical qubits support ~1000 logical qubits \u2014 sufficient for fault-tolerant quantum chemistry (FeMoco at chemical accuracy) and combinatorial optimisation (1000-variable QUBO).</p><p>Available via IBM Quantum Cloud. 200+ research groups. The modular tile architecture enables scaling to 1M qubits (Condor 3, planned 2029) by adding more tiles.</p>",
      "summary": "IBM Condor 2 is a 100,000-qubit superconducting quantum processor (transmon, 4K dilution refrigerator). The world's first 100k-qubit quantum computer. Modular architecture: 100 tiles of 1000 qubits each, coupled via cryogenic microwave links.",
      "date_published": "2027-01-19T00:00:00Z",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "IBM Quantum"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "Physics - 100k-qubit quantum",
        "IBM Research blog"
      ],
      "_aihpc_dataset": {
        "name": "Condor 2 QEC benchmark (d=7,9,11) + FeMoco + QUBO-1000",
        "format": "CSV + Qiskit shots",
        "size": "320 MB",
        "url": "https://quantum-computing.ibm.com/condor-2"
      }
    },
